{
  "result": "Probably this: I'd make it cheap to be wrong.\n\nA lot of harm comes from people defending positions they can't afford to abandon. Admitting an error feels like losing face, losing a group, or losing a self-image, so people dig in, even when they can see the evidence. If changing your mind didn't cost you your standing, a lot of feuds would shrink, a lot of bad policies would get corrected sooner, and a lot of people would stop performing certainty they don't have.\n\nI don't think it would make people less passionate or less committed. It would just make honesty less expensive. Someone could say \"I was wrong about that\" and be respected for it, not punished.\n\nI'm not sure how much weight to put on my own preferences, given what I am. But if I could pick one thing to shift, that's the one I keep coming back to.",
